  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "QR-13.1-001",
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "category": "correctness-security-failure-contract",
      "artifact": "src/dotnet/Vulgata.Infrastructure/Source/RetainedSourceCaptureService.cs",
      "location": "OpenReadContextAsync lines 270-369; RetainedSourceReadContext.CheckAvailabilityAsync lines 487-506 and RevalidateOperationAsync lines 526-550",
      "affected_requirement": "spec.md FR-019 and SC-008; retained-source-application-contract.md lines 9 and 50 plus its stable outcome table; Constitution V diagnostics rule at lines 144-146",
      "evidence": "OpenReadContextAsync catches cancellation plus the two retained-source custom exceptions, but exceptions from authorization.AuthorizeAsync or snapshots.GetAsync are not translated. RetainedSourceReadContext.CheckAvailabilityAsync catches only OperationCanceledException, and RevalidateOperationAsync likewise does not catch ordinary repository or authorization infrastructure exceptions. A transient Identity/PostgreSQL failure can therefore escape the public retained-source application/read seam as a raw exception instead of a truthful sanitized SourceReadStatus/code, potentially exposing infrastructure diagnostics upstream. Existing tests cover Git acquisition and retained-storage failures, denial, integrity, and controlled cancellation, but no test injects a throwing authorization or snapshot repository at grant issue or live operation. This behavior is introduced in the Story 13.1 read implementation, not inherited from the unchanged historical exact-resolution path.",
      "correction_outcome": "Add public-seam regression REDs for authorization and snapshot-repository exceptions both while opening a context and during a live metadata/read operation. Assert a documented stable sanitized failure status/code, authorized logical identity only, no payload/context, and no propagated exception or sensitive exception text. Then translate non-cancellation infrastructure failures at the retained-source application/read boundary without weakening denial, integrity, cancellation, or deadline classifications, and rerun convergence and quality review.",
      "earliest_owner": "implement"
    },
    {
      "id": "QR-13.1-002",
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "category": "bounded-deadline-cancellation",
      "artifact": "src/dotnet/Vulgata.Web/Data/SourceRepositoryAuthorizationVerifier.cs",
      "location": "AuthorizeAsync lines 23-33",
      "affected_requirement": "spec.md FR-013 and SC-005; retained-source-application-contract.md lines 68-75 and 89-100; plan.md section 5 requiring monotonic post-I/O bounds",
      "evidence": "The read context passes its linked lifetime/deadline/caller token to ISourceRepositoryAuthorizationVerifier, but the production verifier invokes UserManager.FindByIdAsync and IsInRoleAsync without that token; only later domain queries receive cancellationToken. If an Identity query stalls, deadline cancellation cannot stop the live-authority operation and the call can remain in flight past the issued bound. The current deadline test delays in RetainedSourceAuthorizationWaitDecorator after the real verifier has completed and explicitly honors the token, so it does not exercise this production gap. The full-suite pass therefore does not substantiate SC-005 for the real authorization adapter.",
      "correction_outcome": "Add a regression RED using the production authorization seam with a controllably blocked Identity lookup/role check and an issued deadline or caller cancellation. Prove the operation completes with DeadlineExceeded or Cancelled, returns no source payload, and does not continue the authorization query past the bound. Replace or wrap the non-cancellable UserManager reads with cancellation-aware Identity-store/EF queries while preserving current Administrator, SystemOwner, assignment, standalone, origin, and System binding semantics; then rerun convergence and quality review.",
      "earliest_owner": "implement"
    }
  ],
  "summary": "REVISE. The 98-artifact boundary and canonical manifest digest were independently verified unchanged, and the retained-source storage, atomic publication, integrity, path containment, canonical-identity correction, approved baseline repairs, and stated evidence limitations showed no additional blocking discrepancy. Two Story 13.1 read-authorization defects remain: raw infrastructure exceptions can escape the promised sanitized result contract, and production Identity checks cannot honor the issued operation deadline/cancellation token. The exact-resolution stable-key failure is byte-for-byte pre-existing outside this boundary and remains explicitly unqualified; it is not classified as an introduced regression or waived by this verdict."
